#
# Device-specific init for malbec.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Active stylus: re-enable pen scanning in the touch controller.
#
# nvt_touch.ko turns the pen OFF at boot and never turns it back on. This is
# not inference -- it is in our own boot log:
#
#   [7.349807] [NVT-ts] nvt_update_firmware 907: Update firmware success!
#   [7.349968] [NVT-ts] nvt_get_fw_info 997: fw_ver=0x1F, fw_type=0x01, PID=0x609E
#   [7.349985] [NVT-ts] nvt_cmd_store 1053: command = 0x7B 0x00      <-- pen off
#
# Boot_Update_Firmware() (nvt_touch.ko @ 0x799c) runs once as delayed work after
# the first firmware update and calls nvt_support_pen_set(0), which emits the
# host command {0x50, 0x7B, 0x00}. Per the public upstream source this driver is
# built from (lenovo-basements/android_kernel_lenovo_sm8750-modules,
# qcom/opensource/touch-drivers/NT36XXX_SPI/nt36xxx_ext_proc.c:41-59):
#
#   0x7B 0x00   pen not supported     <-- what we get
#   0x7B 0x01   pen supported         <-- what we need
#   0x7B 0x02   enter BLE pairing mode
#   0x7B 0x03   stop BLE pairing mode
#
# There is no other path in the whole module that sends a non-zero 0x7B, so on a
# stock AOSP userspace the controller stays in "pen not supported" forever. On
# ZUI the gap is filled from the framework: Lenovo's services.jar patch
# (com.zui.server.input.styluspen.pen.bluetooth.policy.BluetoothPenConnectPolicy
# -> TouchscreenServiceManager.setPenMode(true)) calls the vendor HAL, whose
# setPenMode() is just writeInt("/proc/support_pen", 1). LineageOS ships that HAL
# binary but has no client for it -- nothing in the tree ever calls ITouchscreen.
#
# Writing the node directly is the same operation the HAL performs, without
# adding a binary or a new SELinux domain for a single integer write.
#
# ⚠️ The value is 1, not 2. Session 13 replayed ZUI's captured call sequence and
# ended up writing 2 via setPenBle(true); 2 is BLE *pairing* mode, not pen-on,
# which is why that experiment looked like it did nothing.
#
# Why sys.boot_completed and not earlier: Boot_Update_Firmware is queued from
# probe as delayed work and fires at ~7.3 s, so anything written before that is
# overwritten. boot_completed lands at ~25 s on this device.
#
# Once is enough. The driver keeps the requested state in ts->support_pen_state
# and re-sends it on every resume and after every firmware reset --
# nvt_restore_cmd_func() @ 0x6174 (scheduled from nvt_panel_notifier_callback)
# and the FW-reset branch of nvt_ts_work_func() @ 0x54a4 both replay
# nvt_support_pen_set(), nvt_pen_type_set() and nvt_pen_attached_set() from the
# stored values. So this does not need a watchdog.
#
# NOT set here: /proc/pen_type ({0x7B, 0x04, v}, the pen model/protocol
# selector). Stock only ever writes it from BluetoothPenInputPolicy, using the
# model reported by the BLE pen, and leaves it at 0 until a pen has connected --
# so 0 is the correct default rather than an omission. Hardcoding a model would
# be a guess about which stylus is paired. See work/analysis/S14-nvt-driver-re.md
# section 8 (H2) for the sweep that would settle it if pressure is still absent.
on property:sys.boot_completed=1
    write /proc/support_pen 1
    write /proc/HighReportRate 0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Device mode: the touch controller's scan mode and the thermal policy.
#
# Measured on this unit, 8 s per window, panel rate read back inside every window
# (work/scripts/61-touch-rate-at-144.sh; raw evdev in work/s18/touch-rate/):
#
#   panel  HighReportRate   finger      pen
#   120    0                120 Hz      240 Hz   (Pen_ID 2;87 = model 2, 87 %)
#   120    1                360 Hz      none
#   144    0                185 Hz      none
#   144    1                185 Hz      none
#   144    1 + pen on       112 Hz      none     <- worst state on the device
#
# Two of those rows overturn what this file used to say:
#
#   * The 360 Hz finger rate exists ONLY at 120 Hz. Above 120 the controller caps
#     at ~185 Hz and HighReportRate stops having any effect at all. The old
#     comment here claimed "HighReportRate 1 -> 360 Hz (3x)" as if it were
#     panel-independent; it is 3x of 120, not 3x of the panel.
#   * HighReportRate is a BOOLEAN. Only the literal 1 arms it: HRR=4 at 120 Hz
#     measured 120.5 Hz, indistinguishable from 0. Session 14's "1..4 all
#     saturate at 349-366 Hz" does not reproduce.
#
# So every rate above 120 is strictly worse here — it costs the stylus AND half
# the touch sampling — and the refresh-rate picker is therefore removed from
# Settings entirely (overlay/SettingsOverlayMalbec). Both modes run at 120 Hz and
# differ only in these writes:
#
#   stylus (UI name "Daily"): support_pen 1 + HighReportRate 0 + thermal normal
#   game   (UI name "Game"):  support_pen 0 + HighReportRate 1 + thermal game
#
# ⚠️ Wire values stay "stylus"/"game" although the UI says Daily/Game: the value
# names the digitizer's hardware state, and persist.sys.malbec.touch_mode survives
# an OTA, so renaming it would leave upgraded devices matching no trigger here at
# all -- no /proc writes, pen silently dead. See app/MalbecParts Constants.kt.
#
# The panel half lives in Settings.System peak_refresh_rate and is written by
# MalbecParts. This is the /proc half. It is done here rather than in the app
# because both nodes are labelled proc_lenovo_touch / proc_lenovo_pen -- vendor
# types -- and giving a core domain write access to them would cross the Treble
# boundary the rest of this tree's sepolicy is careful about. vendor_init
# already writes proc_lenovo_pen for the block above; sepolicy/vendor/
# vendor_init.te adds proc_lenovo_touch for the same reason.
#
# `persist.sys.` is not an arbitrary namespace: it is already
# u:object_r:system_prop:s0 (system/sepolicy/private/property_contexts:79) and
# system_app already holds set_prop for it (private/system_app.te:43), so this
# needs no new property type and no new rule.
#
# ── The thermal half ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# vendor.thermal.mode selects which of Lenovo's own tables for THIS chassis
# /vendor/bin/thermal-engine-v2 loads. The plumbing is already in the vendor
# init we ship (init.qcom.rc:516-526): the service's own argument is
# `-c /vendor/etc/thermal-engine-malbec-${vendor.thermal.mode}.conf`, and
# `on property:vendor.thermal.mode=*` stops and restarts it. So one setprop is
# the whole mechanism; sepolicy/vendor/vendor_init.te:45 already grants
# set_prop(vendor_init, vendor_thermal_prop).
#
# ⚠️ `game`, NOT `skynet`. The two differ in the opposite direction from what the
# names suggest, and this was checked against the conf files rather than against
# anyone's summary of them:
#
#   normal   CPU clusters 1/2/3 step down at 42/44/46/48 C
#            (2611 -> 2323 -> 1920 -> 1190 MHz on cl1/2)
#   game     the 42/44/46 C steps are GONE. Full clock until 60 C, then a
#            three-step ramp 60/64/68 C. Battery-current throttle starts 1 C
#            earlier.
#            ⚠️ These three lines used to read "a single step at 48 C. Full clock
#            until 48 C", which described STOCK's game profile. This tree ships
#            its own (configs/thermal/thermal-engine-malbec-game.conf), whose
#            header documents the change: trigger raised 48 -> 60 C and the cliff
#            replaced by a ramp, on the owner's decision. The conf file and this
#            table had disagreed since that change; the conf file is the
#            authority (`thresholds 60000 64000 68000` on MON-MSM-CPU1/2/3).
#            MalbecParts' touch_mode_footer string said 48 C too, and was
#            corrected in the same pass.
#   skynet   onset moves 42 C -> 40 C, adds a whole MON-MSM-CPU0 block that
#            throttles the little cluster (normal never touches it), and every
#            rung is lower. It is SLOWER and cooler, not faster.
#
# Stock's own mapping settles which is which: system/etc/tn_thermal_config.xml
# assigns ~50 real games (PUBG Mobile, Honor of Kings, CoD Mobile, Wild Rift,
# Brawl Stars, ...) to `game`, and assigns `skynet` to exactly two packages,
# `skynet.cputhrottlingtest` and `com.example.ndkdhryv7` -- i.e. skynet is the
# profile ZUI applies when it detects a CPU-throttling BENCHMARK, to hold the
# chassis temperature down while it runs.
#
# Only the two bare property triggers carry the setprop, not the boot_completed
# ones. The bare triggers fire both when persist properties load during
# second-stage init and on every runtime change, so both paths are covered, while
# a `normal` device avoids a second redundant thermal-engine restart per boot
# (properties/vendor.prop already ships vendor.thermal.mode=normal as the
# build-time default, and init notifies on every set even when the value is
# unchanged -- property_service.cpp:427).
#
# ⚠️ ORDER MATTERS, and it is load-bearing. Both this and the plain
# `on property:sys.boot_completed=1` block above fire on the same event, and
# init runs same-trigger actions in parse order (ActionManager iterates its list
# in insertion order, and the parser inserts in file order). So the block above
# sets the stylus default and this one overrides it when the owner chose game
# mode. Reordering these two silently breaks game mode across a reboot.
#
# Why also gate on boot_completed rather than on the property alone: persist
# properties are loaded in second-stage init, long before nvt_touch.ko's
# Boot_Update_Firmware delayed work fires at ~7.3 s and sends {0x50,0x7B,0x00}.
# Anything written before that is overwritten. The bare property triggers below
# handle the runtime case, where the driver is long past that point.
#
# ⚠️ Within each block, support_pen is written BEFORE HighReportRate, and that is
# now load-bearing for a second reason. The measured table above has one state
# that is worse than either mode -- 144 Hz with HighReportRate 1 AND pen scanning
# still on, 112 Hz, below the panel rate. Writing HighReportRate first would rest
# there for the duration of one write during every daily -> game transition.
on property:sys.boot_completed=1 && property:persist.sys.malbec.touch_mode=game
    write /proc/support_pen 0
    write /proc/HighReportRate 1

on property:persist.sys.malbec.touch_mode=game
    write /proc/support_pen 0
    write /proc/HighReportRate 1
    setprop vendor.thermal.mode game

on property:persist.sys.malbec.touch_mode=stylus
    write /proc/support_pen 1
    write /proc/HighReportRate 0
    setprop vendor.thermal.mode normal

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Touch controller edge / palm rejection: follow the display rotation.
#
# The Novatek controller does edge and palm rejection in firmware and needs to
# be told which way up the tablet is. On a plain AOSP userspace nothing tells
# it, so it sits at its power-on default of 0 degrees -- PORTRAIT -- on a
# 13-inch tablet that is normally used in landscape with a keyboard attached.
# Measured on the device with the display at ROTATION_90:
#
#     /proc/panel_direction  ->  Vertical Direction!(0 degree)
#     dumpsys window         ->  mRotation=ROTATION_90
#
# The owner independently reported the symptom before this was written: edge
# rejection was working on the long edges and not on the short ones, which is
# exactly where portrait bands land on a landscape screen.
#
# Stock does this from ZuiPhoneWindowManager's EdgeInhibitionInputPolicy.
# MalbecParts' PanelDirectionController replaces it and publishes the rotation
# here; these four triggers do the write, for the same reason the device-mode
# block above does: /proc/panel_direction is proc_lenovo_touch, a VENDOR type,
# and the app runs in the core system_app domain.
#
# The value needs no translation. nvt_edge_reject_set() sends one byte,
# 0xBA..0xBD, and the node's domain was measured by writing each value and
# reading it back:
#
#     0 -> Vertical Direction!(0 degree)          3 -> Left Up Direction!(270 degree)
#     1 -> Right Up Direction!(90 degree)         4 -> Not Support!
#     2 -> Vertical reverse Direction!(180 degree)
#
# i.e. exactly Surface.ROTATION_0..270, same order, same numbering.
#
# ⚠️ The firmware rejects on ONE PAIR of edges, and panel_direction selects
# which pair -- it does not add bands, it moves them. Owner-verified both ways:
# at 0 the long edges rejected and the short ones did not; at 1 the short edges
# rejected and the long ones did not. So "all four edges" is not reachable
# through this node, and following the rotation is the best available answer
# because it puts the bands where the hands are. `/proc/game_edge` was tried as
# a candidate for the missing pair and made no difference (owner-verified), so
# it stays at its default 0, as does /proc/edge_grid_zone at 0,0,0,0.
#
# No trigger is needed for the boot case: the controller powers up at 0 and
# PanelDirectionController syncs once as soon as it starts.
on property:sys.malbec.panel_dir=0
    write /proc/panel_direction 0

on property:sys.malbec.panel_dir=1
    write /proc/panel_direction 1

on property:sys.malbec.panel_dir=2
    write /proc/panel_direction 2

on property:sys.malbec.panel_dir=3
    write /proc/panel_direction 3

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-unit Bluetooth / Wi-Fi MAC address storage.
#
# These two directories are where this unit's hardware identity lives:
#
#   /mnt/vendor/persist/bluetooth/.bt_nv.bin   -> ro.odm.lenovo.btaddr
#   /mnt/vendor/persist/wcn7750/wlan_mac.bin   -> ro.odm.lenovo.wifiaddr
#
# libLenovoNvram.so reads them, and /vendor/bin/prop_init (class core, enabled,
# started from `on boot`) is what loads that library and publishes the two
# properties.
#
# On stock the only thing that created either directory was tndiag.rc's
# `on boot` block. tndiag is deliberately not shipped here (it is a
# root-privileged DIAG serial-number / MAC / RPMB-key write surface -- the
# reasoning is in sepolicy/vendor/daemons.te), so the mkdirs had to move
# somewhere, and this is that somewhere.
#
# `on post-fs` rather than stock's `on boot`, for two reasons, both improvements
# rather than transcription:
#   * /mnt/vendor/persist is mounted by then -- QTI's own persist mkdirs
#     (init.qcom.rc:167-168 /time and /iar_db, :243 /display, :252 /hvdcp_opti)
#     are in this same action, so the stage is proven on this device.
#   * `on boot` would be a race. init merges all `on boot` blocks and runs them
#     in file-parse order, and prop_init.rc's `on boot / start prop_init` sorts
#     before tndiag.rc did -- so on stock the directories were created *after*
#     the process that reads them was started, and it only ever worked because
#     persist survives flashing and the directories were already there from the
#     factory. Doing it at post-fs removes the dependency on that history, which
#     matters for anyone who ever erases persist.
#
# Modes and ownership are verbatim from stock tndiag.rc.
on post-fs
    mkdir /mnt/vendor/persist/wcn7750 0770 system system
    mkdir /mnt/vendor/persist/bluetooth 0770 system system

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Charging control gate.
#
# The node Settings' "Charging control" drives,
#   /sys/class/power_supply/battery/charge_control_end_threshold
# is inert until this class attribute is 1. The gate is in
# qti_battery_charger.ko (battery_psy_set_charge_end_threshold).
#
# ⚠️ The parking values are not cosmetic. The pair boots holding 70/80 -- ZUI's
# battery-health setting, left behind in the charger firmware's state. The
# moment the gate opens those become live and the tablet stops charging at 80%
# with nothing in the UI to explain it. 100/95 is "no limit": end at the top of
# the driver's [55 100] range, start just under it. The framework re-applies the
# owner's real choice from Settings when charging control is enabled.
#
# ── Two corrections, both measured on the running device ───────────────────
#
# 1. `on boot` DID NOT WORK. Measured after 12 h of uptime, with this block
#    shipped and the file demonstrably parsed (the support_pen write above had
#    taken, /proc/support_pen read "Pen state 1!" against a driver default of 0):
#
#        charge_control_en                 0
#        charge_control_end_threshold     80     <- should have been 100
#        charge_control_start_threshold   70     <- should have been 95
#
#    Not a permissions problem: all three nodes are root-owned 0644 and
#    vendor_init writes them. The charger firmware link (pmic_glink) is simply
#    not up at `on boot`; battery_chg probes earlier but the glink channel is
#    not guaranteed until later, which the previous version of this comment
#    already suspected and shipped anyway. Moved to sys.boot_completed=1, which
#    lands around 25 s -- long after the kernel's own BATTERY_CHG worker starts
#    logging.
#
# 2. The gate is NOT symmetric, and the old comment ("the two nodes ... are
#    inert") had it wrong. Measured, in one sitting, late in the boot:
#
#        echo 1   > charge_control_en          -> reads back 1
#        echo 100 > end_threshold              -> takes (80 -> 100); en falls to 0
#        echo 95  > start_threshold  (en == 0) -> TAKES ANYWAY (70 -> 95)
#
#    So `end_threshold` is gated and one-shot; `start_threshold` is not gated at
#    all. That also refines PROGRESS.md's error #74, which modelled the latch as
#    covering both. The practical consequence is that one `en=1` per
#    `end_threshold` write is enough and no re-open is needed for the start
#    value -- but the gate is written immediately before the end write rather
#    than at the top of the block, so the pairing is visible and a future
#    reorder cannot silently separate them.
#
# ✔ VERIFIED, session 22, on the flashed build:
#     charge_control_end_threshold    100
#     charge_control_start_threshold   95
#     charge_control_en                 0
# i.e. exactly what the latch analysis above predicted, down to `en` falling back
# to 0 after the gated end_threshold write consumed it. The block runs, both
# thresholds take, and no "Unable to write to file" warning appears in the boot
# capture. Nothing further to check here.
#
# (If a future flash reads 80 and 70 instead, this block did not run: look at
# `adb logcat -b kernel -d | grep -i charge_control` and at init's own warnings.)
#
# ── ★ The second half of charging control: the HAL could not open its node ──
#
# device.mk wires LineageOS charging control to input_suspend and
# sepolicy/vendor/hal_lineage_health.te grants
# rw_dir_file(hal_lineage_health_default, vendor_sysfs_battery_supply) against
# the label the node really carries. Both halves are right, and the feature was
# still completely dead, because nobody checked the file MODE:
#
#     /sys/class/power_supply/battery/input_suspend   -rw-rw-r--  root root
#     vendor.lineage.health-service.default           uid 1000, gid 1000,
#                                                     no supplementary groups,
#                                                     CapEff 0
#
# uid 1000 is neither the owner nor in the owning group, so it lands on `other`
# = r--. Measured, with the feature switched on from the framework:
#
#     E vendor.lineage.health-service.default:
#         Failed to write to charging enable node: Permission denied
#
# and input_suspend stayed 0 with the battery still charging past the limit.
#
# ⚠️ Stock DOES have a fix and it does not work either. init.target.rc:191 is
# `chmod 0666 /sys/class/power_supply/battery/input_suspend`, in `on boot` —
# and the battery power_supply device does not exist yet at `on boot`, which is
# the identical reason the charge_control block below had to move here. Proof:
# every `chmod 0666` in that stock block that targets a battery node reads 0664
# at steady state, while the one targeting panel0-backlight/hbm (created at
# t≈2.07 s) took. ZUI never noticed because it does not use this feature.
#
# ✔ Proven on the hardware, four steps with a control:
#     enable, no chown  -> Permission denied ×2, input_suspend 0, "Charging"
#     + chown, mode AUTO-> no error; controller reports "No alarm found"
#     + mode LIMIT      -> input_suspend 1, status "Not charging", usb online 1,
#                          mIsLimitSet true, notification posted, zero errors
#     restored          -> back to 0 / "Charging"
#
# `chown`, not stock's `chmod 0666`: the only writer on this ROM is one uid-1000
# HAL, so owner access is sufficient and a world-writable charge-inhibit node is
# not a thing to inherit. Same shape and the same trigger as the hbm chown
# below — OPEN-ISSUES #35a, whose lesson this node did not get.
#
# ⚠️ Fast charging is untouched: this writes nothing to restrict_chg/restrict_cur,
# and input_suspend stays 0 with the feature off, which is the default.
on property:sys.boot_completed=1
    chown system system /sys/class/power_supply/battery/input_suspend
    write /sys/class/qcom-battery/charge_control_en 1
    write /sys/class/power_supply/battery/charge_control_end_threshold 100
    write /sys/class/power_supply/battery/charge_control_start_threshold 95

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ★ Reported charging speed: hide the UCSI *source* power supply from the health
# HAL, which is what Qualcomm's own health HAL already asks for and does not get.
# Session 24, OPEN-ISSUES #52.
#
# Symptom, measured on this build against the SAME unit's stock dump, on the SAME
# 900 mA PC port:
#
#     ours   dumpsys battery -> Max charging current: 3000000   ("Charging rapidly")
#     stock  dumpsys battery -> Max charging current:  900000   ("Charging")
#
# 3 A x 5 V = 15 W is over AOSP's 7.5 W "fast" threshold, so the lock screen, the
# battery Settings page and every app reading EXTRA_MAX_CHARGING_CURRENT are told
# the tablet is fast-charging off a USB2 port that can supply 4.5 W.
#
# It is not a kernel difference. Both sysfs trees are identical — stock's own
# power_supply dump (work/device_dump/stock/power/power_supply.txt:72-80) has the
# same two USB-type supplies with the same values:
#
#     ucsi-source-psy-…:ucsi1   type=USB  online=1  current_max=3000000
#     usb                       type=USB  online=1  current_max= 900000
#
# The first is the Type-C *advertised source capability*; the second is what the
# port is actually allowed to draw (its input_current_limit reads 500000).
# BatteryMonitor::updateValues() (system/core/healthd/BatteryMonitor.cpp:477-577)
# enumerates every online charger supply and keeps the one with the highest
# current_max x voltage_max, so the advertisement wins over the reality.
#
# ★★ Qualcomm already fixed this and AOSP undoes it. healthd-ext's own board init
# names this exact PSY:
#
#     vendor/qcom/opensource/healthd-ext/aidl/main.cpp:33-36,47-48
#         constexpr char *ucsiPSYName[]{ "ucsi-source-psy-soc:qcom,pmic_glink:qcom,ucsi1",
#                                        "…ucsi2" };
#         hc->ignorePowerSupplyNames.push_back(String8(ucsiPSYName[0]));
#
# and BatteryMonitor honours that list in init() (:862-866) — but updateValues()
# does `mChargerNames.clear()` and rescans /sys/class/power_supply from scratch
# (:484-517) WITHOUT consulting it, so the very first update puts the ignored
# supply straight back. The ignore list works exactly once, before any reading is
# taken. That is an upstream AOSP defect, not a Qualcomm or a Lenovo one.
#
# The device-tree lever is the gate updateValues() uses to build its list:
#
#     path = ".../<name>/online";
#     if (access(path, R_OK) == 0) mChargerNames.add(name);
#
# access() is DAC, and the health HAL runs as uid 1000 (`ps -A -o USER,NAME` ->
# system  android.hardware.health-service.qti), so making `online` root-only
# removes this one supply from the scan and leaves every other supply, and every
# other reader, untouched. It is the narrowest expression of
# ignorePowerSupplyNames that a device tree can write.
#
# ✔ Proven on the hardware, four steps with a control:
#     chmod 0600 online + restart HAL   -> Max charging current:  900000
#     chmod 0444 online + restart HAL   -> Max charging current: 3000000
#   (the restart is required — BatteryService caches the last broadcast, so a
#    chmod alone changes nothing until the HAL takes a new reading.)
#
# Trigger: boot_completed, not `on boot`. The supply is created by pmic_glink at
# kernel ≈4.41 s and the vendor `on boot` block runs at ≈4.07 s — the same reason
# the charge-threshold block above had to move. Between boot and boot_completed
# the value is briefly 3 A; nothing reads it that early.
#
# Enforcing: covered with no new rule. vendor_sysfs_usb_supply is declared
# `sysfs_type, fs_type` (device/qcom/sepolicy_vndr/sm8750/generic/vendor/common/
# file.te:107) and AOSP grants vendor_init `fs_type:file setattr`.
#
# ⚠️ If this ever needs reverting, the node is only used for this: drop the line,
# and `Max charging current` goes back to reporting the Type-C advertisement.
# The clean fix is four lines upstream (make updateValues() consult
# ignorePowerSupplyNames the way init() does); this tree does not patch upstream.
#
# (Its own action rather than a line appended to the charging-control block
# above: the two are independent, and this file already documents that ordering
# between same-trigger blocks is load-bearing where it matters.)
on property:sys.boot_completed=1
    chmod 0600 /sys/class/power_supply/ucsi-source-psy-soc:qcom,pmic_glink:qcom,ucsi1/online

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LiveDisplay Outdoor mode: wait for the HBM node before `class hal` starts.
#
# vendor.lineage.livedisplay-service.sysfs is `class hal`
# (hardware/lineage/interfaces/livedisplay/aidl/sysfs/
#  vendor.lineage.livedisplay-service.sysfs.rc:17-21), and on flash 1 it lost the
# race and SIGABRTed on every boot:
#
#     Abort message: 'None of SunlightEnhancement paths could be opened'
#     #03 SunlightEnhancement::SunlightEnhancement()+364
#
# That constructor is not tolerant: SunlightEnhancement.cpp:42-53 probes its
# candidate paths with access(R_OK|W_OK) and calls LOG(FATAL) if none opens.
# init restarts the service four seconds later and the second attempt succeeds,
# so the ONLY visible cost today is one tombstone and one F-level log per boot —
# which is why it survived flash 1 unnoticed while the DisplayModes crash next
# door did not.
#
# ⚠️ It is not merely cosmetic, and that is the reason to fix it rather than
# document it. ISunlightEnhancement is declared in the VINTF manifest, so it is
# subject to exactly the trap in OPEN-ISSUES #34: a declared HAL that never
# registers is an untimed waitForDeclaredService() on a system_server thread. One
# retry is the difference between "self-heals" and "the boot hangs", and nothing
# guarantees the second attempt wins either.
#
# Timing, measured on flash 1 rather than assumed:
#     kernel 2.129  [drm:sde_backlight_setup] creates panel0-backlight
#     kernel ~3.5   class hal starts the service -> access() fails -> SIGABRT
#     kernel  8.475 the restarted instance writes hbm successfully (AVC, permissive)
# so the backlight DEVICE exists a second before the service runs and the `hbm`
# ATTRIBUTE does not. The window is somewhere in 3.5-7.5 s; the restart delay
# hides where.
#
# `on early-boot` is the last trigger before `on boot`
# (system/core/rootdir/init.rc:588-589), and `class_start hal` is inside `on boot`
# (:1284), so this runs immediately before the service is started and nowhere
# else.
#
# ⚠️ THE RACE WAS NOT THE CAUSE. The `wait` below was written as a fix and turned
# out to be only the instrument; the line it prints is what disproved the theory:
#
#     init: wait for '/sys/class/backlight/panel0-backlight/hbm' took 0ms
#
# 0 ms. The node is already there. Trigger order, measured by writing markers to
# /dev/kmsg from each trigger in turn and reading them back out of dmesg:
#
#     [2.075] sde_backlight_setup creates panel0-backlight
#     [2.299] on late-fs        [2.876] on post-fs-data
#     [3.347] on early-boot  <- the wait runs here and finds the node
#     [3.664] on boot        <- our marker; init.rc's own `on boot` (:1284,
#                               class_start hal) runs BEFORE it, because init.rc
#                               is parsed first and same-trigger actions run in
#                               parse order
#     ro.boottime.vendor.livedisplay-sysfs = 3.450 s   <- between the two, i.e.
#                               class_start hal, exactly as expected
#
# So the file existed 100 ms before the service opened it, and access() still
# failed. It is a PERMISSION problem wearing a race's clothes.
#
# ★ What actually fixes it is one line, and it is the line below the wait.
# Measured, four boots, one variable at a time:
#
#     wait only ............................ 1 SIGABRT   (repeated, 2 boots)
#     wait + chown + chmod 0666 ............ 0
#     wait + chown ......................... 0
#     wait only (control, re-removed) ...... 1 SIGABRT   <- causality
#
# Mechanism: the service runs as uid `system` and probes with
# access(path, R_OK|W_OK) (SunlightEnhancement.cpp:42-53). At steady state the
# node reads `0666 root:root`, on which that call from `system` cannot fail — so
# the node must be MORE RESTRICTIVE at 3.45 s and be relaxed later by the display
# driver. `chown system system` makes the HAL's own uid the owner, so the owner
# write bit applies to it from the first attempt instead of the second. The exact
# early mode was not observed directly (init has no way to report it), so that
# last step is inference from the four measurements above rather than a reading.
#
# This is also exactly what upstream does for the nodes it does know about:
# hardware/lineage/interfaces/livedisplay/aidl/sysfs/
# vendor.lineage.livedisplay-service.sysfs.rc:6-16 chowns `system system` onto
# /sys/devices/.../dsi-display-primary/hbm and the fb0 tuneables. This device is
# DRM-only with no fb0 and its HBM control lives on the backlight class device,
# so none of those paths match here and the chown never reached ours.
#
# Why it still matters even though init restarts the service four seconds later
# and the second attempt succeeds: ISunlightEnhancement is DECLARED in the VINTF
# manifest, and a declared HAL that never registers is an untimed
# waitForDeclaredService() on system_server's boot thread — the exact hang
# OPEN-ISSUES #34 describes. One retry is the difference between "self-heals" and
# "the boot stops", and nothing promises the second attempt wins either.
#
# The `wait` stays. It costs 0 ms on this hardware, it is what makes the chown
# meaningful (chowning a node that does not exist yet is a no-op and init only
# warns), and if a future kernel ever does move the node later, the number it
# prints says so immediately. Two seconds, not more, because `wait` blocks init's
# whole action queue.
on early-boot
    wait /sys/class/backlight/panel0-backlight/hbm 2
    chown system system /sys/class/backlight/panel0-backlight/hbm
